## What the project remembers, and where

There is no session-wide activity log here, deliberately. Capturing every prompt
and every tool call runs to tens of megabytes a project, will not go in git, and
answers none of the questions people actually ask. "What did my coauthors do
last week", "why did we change that", and "did we ever explore X" are questions
about *decisions*, and a tool-call log records actions. So the decision gets
written down, with its reason, at the moment it is made.

Two committed records carry the project, and between them they answer all three
questions:

1. **`project/global/state.md` and its changelog.** One entry at the end of
   every round — what the round took up, and what came of it — plus any
   substantive change as it happens. Newest first, append only. Killed ideas go
   in with the reason they were killed. This is how the others see what moved
   without reading the whole file, and it is the first place to look for any of
   the three questions above.

   The round entry is written **even when the round settled nothing**. A round
   that took something up and dropped it leaves no diff and no artifact, so if
   its entry is not written the exploration is simply gone.
2. **`project/<author>/logs/runs.jsonl`.** One line per script execution, with
   inputs, outputs, and the commit. This is what ties a number to the run that
   produced it.

Git history sits underneath both, and `git log --since` answers "last week"
directly.

Everything else in `logs/` is working material and is gitignored: the briefs
sent to each debate seat, `logs/debate-<run>.jsonl` with the full model
responses, and anything a tool wrote to a scratch path. Read a slice when a
specific question needs one —

```bash
python3 -m tools.logging_ --seat adversary --limit 5
```

— but none of it is memory. A brief is the input to a decision, not the
decision, and "did we ever explore X" is answered by the round entries in the
changelog, not by a folder of prompts somebody would have to reverse-engineer. If a voice said something that changed the project's
direction, that belongs in `state.md` with the reason.

**When you learn something the project should keep, write it down now.** Not at
the end of the session, not in your own words in a scratch file — into
`state.md` with a changelog line, or into the run record. Nothing reconstructs
it later.

A run is stamped with `python3 -m tools.runid --project . --new` at the top of a
round. Files are named by run, never by round: a round counter is per-machine,
so in a shared repo two people each have a round 3, and `<author>-<date>-<time>`
cannot collide.

