## One round at a time — /round

A **round** is one bounded stretch of work that ends with `state.md` rewritten.
`state.md` is long prose rewritten at every gate, and git cannot merge that, so
exactly one person holds it at a time. A push is atomic, which makes it a usable
mutex: exactly one claim wins, and the loser finds out at claim time instead of
after a round's work.

```
/round                                   finish one: write the entry, promote, release
/round start "peer rule for microcaps"   optional — claim it first
/round status                            who holds it, and is the changelog behind
```

**Bare `/round` is the command.** It works whether or not a round was ever
claimed; when none was, work out what was taken up from the conversation, say it
back for correction, and skip the release. Never tell someone to go back and
run `start` — that is a two-person feature, and nobody working alone should be
made to perform it.

`start` earns its keep when a coauthor might be working right now: claiming up
front surfaces a collision in the first minute rather than after you have both
spent an afternoon on the same question. Claim before reading state, and release
after writing it even if the round was killed — an unreleased lock blocks
everyone, and it fails silently.

Underneath, that is `python3 -m tools.lock claim|status|release`; use `/round`
rather than the raw tool so the promotion and the changelog are not skipped.

**A round is not a session.** A round is a unit of *work* and can span several
context windows; a session is one conversation. A long round survives any number
of `/clear`s. Do not end a round because the context filled up, and do not keep
one open for days because nothing forced you to close it.
